>
    <p class="lede">
        Patients often leave without a clear, traceable record of what was
        done to the body. She put post-care capture at 16 percent.
    </p>
    <p>
        For almost two decades she has worked across aesthetic medicine,
        aesthetic surgery, and professional postgraduate education for doctors
        and surgeons. The clinic may keep the file. If the patient changes
        providers, travels, or develops a complication, the next clinician may
        not know the product, the device, the implant, or the procedure. A
        patient may know they had a facelift, and still not know which kind.
        She means the verified record of care already delivered, not a
        provider's plan for a future treatment.
    </p>
    <p>
        The project she is working on asks whether every person can have
        continuous access to a verified treatment history while privacy and
        clinical integrity stay protected. She called that one of the clearest
        practical cases for patient data sovereignty and governed private AI.
    </p>
    <p>Two examples from her own experience.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Vaccinations for her two children, from birth to college, sometimes
            at school and sometimes with the GP. Providers handed over
            different booklets, and some handed over none. Years later a parent
            may not know where every booklet is, and may still have to prove
            the history for college or travel.
        </li>
        <li>
            Aesthetic medicine and surgery: Botox, fillers, breast implants, a
            facelift. Patients may be unable to name the product or the
            technique. After medical tourism, a complication can show up only
            once they are home. The doctors taking over may have no reliable
            record, and the original provider may be hard to reach or unwilling
            to share it.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Sovereignty, as she set it out, protects both patient control and
        clinical integrity. The patient receives and keeps a verified record
        of significant care, can understand it, decides who may see it, and
        carries it across providers and borders. The treating professional
        authenticates the entry. The patient cannot simply alter it. A
        correction is a signed and dated addition that keeps the original and
        its audit trail.
    </p>
    <p>
        The record might hold vaccinations, allergies, medications,
        supplements, the latest blood tests, ECG reports, and bone density
        measurements, plus procedures, implants, devices, and autologous
        tissue or cell-based treatments.
    </p>
    <p>
        Governed AI can organize that verified information into a timeline,
        explain the clinical language, and prepare a short summary for the
        next provider or for the patient. It can surface what needs a
        professional to look: missing treatment details, possible supplement
        duplications, potential interactions, excessive cumulative intake,
        results that matter to a planned treatment, and selected, clinically
        validated relationships between genetics and medicines. These are
        prompts for review. The AI should never independently diagnose,
        prescribe, or tell someone to stop a treatment. Every output links
        back to the source. Clinically important findings require a person.
        Private AI can make the sensitive record useful while access stays
        under the patient's control.
    </p>
    <p>
        She would begin with a clearly defined problem and a practical
        demonstration, one that can produce credible evidence without promises
        made before they have been tested. A project will begin in Europe,
        starting in Ireland. She wants a wider movement around patient data
        sovereignty and responsibly governed AI in healthcare, and she
        welcomed connections to Canada and to similar projects.
    </p>
