            <p>
                For almost two decades, I have worked across aesthetic
                medicine, aesthetic surgery, and professional postgraduate
                education for doctors and surgeons. Through this work, I keep
                seeing a specific patient safety risk after treatment. For
                example, patients often do not have a clear, traceable record
                of what was actually done to their body. They might have some
                idea, but data shows that most post-care patients capture only
                16 percent of it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The clinic may retain the information, but if the patient
                changes providers, travels, or develops complications, the next
                clinician may not know what product, device, implant, or
                procedure was actually used on them. A patient might vaguely
                know, okay, I had a facelift, for example, but they're not
                really clear what kind of facelift they had. So, I'm referring
                to the verified, factual record of care already delivered,
                rather than a provider's proposed treatment plan for a future
                clinical strategy.
            </p>
            <p>
                Now, I'm working on a project focused on this step: can every
                person have continuous access to a verified treatment history
                while privacy and clinical integrity remain protected? This is
                one of the major questions that we have to ask ourselves, and I
                believe this is one of the clearest practical cases for patient
                data sovereignty and governed private AI.
            </p>
            <p>
                Let me give you two examples from my own experience. One is not
                related to aesthetics, and the other is just vaccinations for
                my two children. My two children received vaccinations
                throughout their lives, from birth until they were 18 years old
                and able to go to college. The vaccinations were provided
                sometimes by the school, and sometimes directly with the GP.
                During these vaccinations, every provider gave us a booklet.
                Some didn't even give us a booklet. So as a parent, you are
                receiving different booklets, but there was no single
                consolidated record. Years later, a parent may not remember
                where every booklet is, which provider administered each
                vaccine, or how to retrieve a complete history. And yet, when
                my kids are going to college or traveling, I may have to prove
                the types of vaccinations they have received, or any other kind
                of medical care they might have had.
            </p>
            <p>
                Another example is in aesthetic medicine and aesthetic surgery
                procedures. Say, for example, if you go for Botox, fillers,
                breast implants, or facelift procedures, fragmentation can
                become a critical safety problem. Patients may be unable to
                identify the fillers, implants, or devices used on them. They
                may not know the exact product or precisely what techniques
                were performed. This becomes especially serious after medical
                tourism. A complication may develop only after patients return
                home, and the doctors taking over the care may have no reliable
                record, while the original provider may be hard to contact or
                unwilling to share that piece of information.
            </p>
            <p>
                Patient data sovereignty must protect both patient control and
                clinical integrity. The patient should receive and retain a
                verified record of significant care actually delivered to their
                body. They should be able to understand it, decide who may
                access it, and carry it across providers and borders.
            </p>
            <p>
                The treating professional remains responsible for authenticating
                the clinical entry, and the patient cannot simply alter it.
                When an error requires corrections, a signed and dated addition
                should preserve the original entry and its audit trail.
            </p>
            <p>
                The record might include vaccinations, allergies, medications,
                supplements, latest blood test results, ECG reports, and bone
                density measurements. All of this information a patient should
                be able to access easily. It could also cover procedures they
                have received, any kind of implants newly put in their bodies,
                any devices used, or any autologous tissue or cell-based
                treatments they have recently received.
            </p>
            <p>
                A portable record becomes more useful when people can understand
                it. Governed AI can organize verified information into a
                timeline, explain clinical language, and prepare a concise
                summary for the next provider or for the patient. It could
                surface matters requiring professional review, such as missing
                treatment details, possible supplement duplications, potential
                interactions, excessive cumulative intake, or results relevant
                to planned treatments. It could also survey selected, clinically
                validated relationships between your genetics and medicines.
            </p>
            <p>
                These are prompts for review. The AI should never independently
                diagnose, prescribe, or tell someone to stop such treatments.
                Every output should link back to the source, and clinically
                important findings require human review. Private AI can make
                sensitive information useful while keeping access under the
                patient's control.
            </p>
            <p>
                So, where do we begin? We begin with a clearly defined problem
                and a practical demonstration, one that can produce credible
                evidence without making promises before they have been tested.
                A verified, portable health record provides a practical space
                to start: can a patient carry a verified record of care they
                have received between providers while controlling access and
                preserving the integrity of the clinical record?
            </p>
            <p>
                I'm working on a project that will begin in Europe, starting in
                Ireland. I also intend to help build a wider movement around
                patient data sovereignty and responsibly governed AI in
                healthcare. If this project and its wider vision are of any
                interest to you, I would be pleased to continue the
                conversation, and I would particularly welcome connections to
                Canada and similar projects. Thank you.
            </p>
